  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Wednesday through Friday Fog & Low Cloud Potential: Strong \n   high pressure increasing likelihood of valley inversions.\n\n-  Pattern Shift: A Pacific system will tap into atmospheric river\n   moisture beginning Saturday, snow levels stay relatively high.\n\n-  Further cooling early to mid next week with potential for snow\n   levels to lower further.\n\nA lingering plume of atmospheric moisture will maintain showers \nthrough tonight, mainly north of Interstate 90. Snow levels remain\nhigh and thus accumulating snow will only occur generally above \n6500 feet.\n\nHigh pressure begins to strengthen over the region late tonight \nand will likely last into Friday. The main forecast challenge will\nbe for the development of stratus and low fog. Forecast models \nfor the most part indicate enough cloud cover will linger across \nthe region tonight to keep widespread fog and stratus at bay. \nHowever, any areas that do see a period of clearing, especially \nthat have received measurable rainfall over the last 24 hours, \nwill likely see fog or stratus develop. As high pressure \nstrengthens further Wednesday into Thursday, the potential for \nmore widespread low clouds and fog to develop and linger will \nincrease for the valleys of western Montana and north central \nIdaho. Locations that do see strong inversions with stratus or fog\nwill likely not warm as much as the current forecast reflects. \nThis will be fine-tuned over the next few days as stratus and fog \ndevelop.\n\nBy Friday night into the weekend, forecast models indicate\nsouthwest flow will form over the region in response to a broad\nareas of low pressure along the west coast. This feed will likely\ntap into another plume of atmospheric moisture to bring\nprecipitation and cooler temperatures to the region. The best\nplume of moisture is currently focused along and south of\nInterstate 90. Snow levels will still remain relatively high\n(5500-6000 feet) but could be low enough to allow for some\naccumulations at high elevations such as Lost Trail Pass,\nGeorgetown Lake, and MacDonald/ Homestake Passes.\n\nEnsemble guidance favors a split-flow pattern through much of \nnext week. Successive upper-level troughs and atmospheric rivers \nwill crash into the West Coast, but the bulk of the dynamic energy\nwill dive south into California and the Desert Southwest. \nConsequently, the Northern Rockies can expect generally seasonable\ntemperatures (though 10 to 15 degrees cooler than recent history)\nand relatively light precipitation.",
